{"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The suffix automaton of a text: the smallest deterministic automaton that accepts every suffix","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of it, whose paths from the start state spell exactly the text’s substrings (Blumer et al.,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"“The Smallest Automaton Recognizing the Subwords of a Text”, 1985).","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SuffixStructures\/documentation\/SuffixStructures\/SuffixAutomaton"},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"SuffixAutomaton","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Symbol","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":"> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"where","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Symbol","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Hashable","preciseIdentifier":"s:SH","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"overview","level":2,"text":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"It is built one symbol at a time in O(","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"text":") expected steps, with at most 2","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"text":" − 1 states and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"3","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"text":" − 4 transitions. Each state stands for the substrings that end at the same set of places","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in the text; its suffix link leads to the state of the longest of their suffixes that ends at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"more places. A pattern is checked in O(","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"m","type":"text"}]},{"text":") transitions.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let automaton = SuffixAutomaton(\"abcbc\")","automaton.contains(\"cbc\")              \/\/ true","automaton.occurrenceCount(of: \"bc\")    \/\/ 2","automaton.firstOccurrence(of: \"cb\")    \/\/ 2 ..< 4","automaton.distinctSubstringCount       \/\/ 12","automaton.longestCommonSubstring(with: \"xbcbz\")?.textRange  \/\/ 1 ..< 4, \"bcb\""],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Transitions from a state come in the order of their symbols: sorted when "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Symbol"},{"type":"text","text":" is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Comparable"},{"type":"text","text":", otherwise in the order the symbols first occur in the text. The automaton is a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"directed graph, "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/SuffixStructures\/documentation\/SuffixStructures\/SuffixAutomaton\/transitionsView-swift.property"},{"type":"text","text":", and its suffix links are a tree that converts to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Grafluent’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Arborescence"},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Arborescence(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/suffixstructures\/suffixautomaton"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SuffixStructures\/documentation\/SuffixStructures"]]},"metadata":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"SuffixAutomaton","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"SuffixAutomaton","externalID":"s:16SuffixStructures0A9AutomatonV","role":"symbol","roleHeading":"Structure","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/GrafluentCollections\/blob\/main\/Sources\/SuffixStructures\/SuffixAutomaton.swift#L24","fileName":"S